@model LoginViewModel
@{ ViewData["Title"] = "Sign in"; }
<section class="auth-section"><div class="auth-photo"><div class="auth-photo-overlay"></div><div class="auth-photo-copy"><div class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-line"></span> GOOD TO HAVE YOU BACK</div><h2>Pick up where<br>your <em>journey</em> left off.</h2><p>Exceptional cars. Thoughtful service. One good conversation away.</p></div></div><div class="auth-panel"><a class="back-link" asp-controller="Home" asp-action="Index">← Back home</a><div class="auth-form-wrap"><div class="eyebrow dark"><span class="eyebrow-line"></span> MEMBER ACCESS</div><h1>Welcome <em>back.</em></h1><p class="muted">Sign in to continue your AutoHaven experience.</p><form asp-action="Login" method="post" class="form-stack"><input type="hidden" name="returnUrl" value="@ViewBag.ReturnUrl"><div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly" class="validation-summary"></div><label asp-for="Email">Email address</label><input asp-for="Email" placeholder="you@example.com" autocomplete="email"><span asp-validation-for="Email" class="field-error"></span><label asp-for="Password">Password</label><input asp-for="Password" type="password" placeholder="Your password" autocomplete="current-password"><span asp-validation-for="Password" class="field-error"></span><button class="btn btn-dark btn-wide" type="submit">Sign in <span>↗</span></button></form><p class="auth-switch">New to AutoHaven? <a asp-action="Register">Create an account</a></p></div></div></section>
